To create a LaTeX document that renders a chessboard based on a FEN representation of a chess board, follow these steps:

1. Choose a chess FEN and include it in your LaTeX document. As an example, let's use the FEN standard:

Standard FEN for a blank chess board:

```
rnbk1nr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBK1NR w KQkq
```

This FEN encodes a position where white pieces own the first six ranks (the upper-half) and black pieces own the lower-half of an empty board with standard chess notation file-colour (r-n-b-k-1-n-r, light-square-colour). The board is said to be in a position with white as the "active" player with the king on e1 square and no en passant capture possible, with a king move prefix (KQkq) indicating that black pieces can still move their king to (q-2) or (k-4) squares, and a white side to play (denoted by the letter `w`).

For simplicity, let's use a FEN for an empty chess board with the default white/light square color and standard starting ranks for both sides:

Latex document:

1. Add the required LaTeX packages for generating a chessboard using the `chessboard` package:

   ```latex
   \documentclass{article}
   \usepackage[version=4]{chessboard}
   \usepackage[tablecolors]{xcolor}
   ```

   - `\documentclass{article}` sets the LaTeX document class. It isn't related to generating a chessboard, but is required for a minimal working LaTeX file.
   - `\usepackage[version=4]{chessboard}` loads the `chessboard` package to generate a chessboard and provides the latest version of the package.

2. Load the necessary font encoding to support Unicode characters (LaTeX works with Unicode by default):

   ```latex
   \usepackage[tablecolors]{xcolor}
   ```

3. Define the starting FEN position for a blank board with white as the side to move (indicated by `w`):

   ```latex
   \newcommand{\blankfens}{rnbk1nr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBK1NR w KQkq}
   ```

4. Create a new LaTeX example that generates a chessboard based on the defined starting position:

   ```latex
   \begin{document}
   \newfont{\chessfont}{ChessBoard-Regular.ttf}
   \pagenumber[1]{}
   \color{white}
   \chessboard{\blankfens}
   \end{document}
   ```

   - `\begin{document}` marks the start of the main document body.
   - `\newfont{\chessfont}{ChessBoard-Regular.ttf}` sets the font to use for the chessboard. Replace `ChessBoard-Regular.ttf` with the path to your font file (which contains the chessboard characters defined in the following step).
   - `\pagename{}` is replaced with `\pagernumber{}` to include the page number if you wish to have one (optional).
   - `\color{white}` sets the background color to white for better visibility.
   - `\chessboard{\blankfens}` actually generates the chessboard using the previously defined `\blankfens` FEN.

5. Define the font characters for chess pieces and board squares (optional, example values only):

   ```latex
   \newcommand{\chw}{\textcolor{white}{\char4}}
   \newcommand{\chb}{\textcolor{white}{\char11}}
   \newcommand{\chk}{\textcolor{white}{\char12}}
   \newcommand{\chN}{\textcolor{white}{\char30}}
   \newcommand{\chB}{\textcolor{white}{\char31}}
   \newcommand{\chR}{\textcolor{white}{\char32}}
   \newcommand{\chP}{\textcolor{white}{\char41}}
   \newcommand{\chQ}{\textcolor{white}{\char42}}
   \newcommand{\chK}{\textcolor{white}{\char4B}}
   \newcommand{\chlightsquare}{\textcolor{black}{\char204D}}
   \newcommand{\chdarksquare}{\textcolor{white}{\char204D}}
   \DeclareFontShape{UTF8}{chessboard}{M}{l}{%
    <5>  [chlightsquare,chdarksquare:620:]  % 5 is the size for a square at 20x20 grid (you can adjust the size as needed)
   }{}
   \DeclareFontShape{UTF8}{chessboard}{M}{ul}{%
    <5>  [chK,chlightsquare,chdarksquare:620:]
       <7>  [chQ,chlightsquare,chdarksquare:570:] % 7 is the size for a square at 25x25 grid (you can adjust the size as needed)
       <9>  [chR,chB,chP,chN,chE,chlightsquare,chdarksquare:510:] % 9 is the size for a square at 30x30 grid (you can adjust the size as needed)
       <10> [chR,chB,chP,chN,chE,chlightsquare,chdarksquare:480:]  % 10 is the size for a square at 35x35 grid (you can adjust the size as needed)
       <12> [chR,chB,chP,chN,chE,chlightsquare,chdarksquare:420:] % 12 is the size for a square at 40x40 grid (you can adjust the size as needed)
       <14> [chR,chB,chP,chN,chE,chlightsquare,chdarksquare:380:] % 14 is the size for a square at 45x45 grid (you can adjust the size as needed)
       <16> [chR,chB,chP,chN,chE,chlightsquare,chdarksquare:340:] % 16 is the size for a square at 50x50 grid (you can adjust the size as needed)
       <999> [chchw,chchb,chchk,chchN,chchE,chchlightsquare,chchdarksquare] % sizes above 999 are for rare cases (optional, exact values don't matter here)
   }{}
   \newfont{\chessfont}{ChessBoard-Regular.ttf}
   \pagenumber{1}
   \color{tablecolors}
   \chessboard{\blankfens}
